{=============================================================================
  Water heated in a spring-loaded piston-cylinder
  CoolSolve Library model CSL-0210
 -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A frictionless piston rests on stops, then meets a linear spring.
  The cylinder holds 4 kg of saturated water at 35 °C. Heat is added
  until the pressure is 7 MPa. The model finds the final state and the
  boundary work on the piston. Exercise 4.21, French edition of Cengel.
  The equation window does not compute the heat transfer.

  Inputs : A_p, k_ressort, m_eau, Vol_1, T_1, p_2, Vol_3, p_4
  Outputs: W, Vol_4, T_4, v_4, x_4
  Units  : SI - temperatures in °C, pressures in Pa, energies in J
  Source : ULiège THD teaching file; the file itself names nobody - see README.md
=============================================================================}

"Exercise 4.21. A frictionless piston of area 0.06 m^2 rests on stops; the enclosed volume is then 30 L.
 The piston mass is such that 300 kPa is required to lift it at ambient pressure. When the volume reaches
 75 L the piston meets a spring that needs 360 kN to compress by 1 m. The cylinder initially holds 4 kg
 of saturated water (liquid and vapour) at 35 °C. The system is heated until the pressure is 7 MPa.
 Determine the final state of the water and the work of the process."

"As in the original: heating lifts the piston, which delivers work. That work is negative for the fluid.
 Each stage is computed as a positive integral of P dV, and the total is signed at the end."

"Data"
A_p = 0.06 [m^2]            "piston area [m^2]"
k_ressort = 360e3 [N/m]    "spring stiffness [N/m]; 360 kN/m in the original"
m_eau = 4 [kg]              "mass of water [kg]"

Vol_1 = 30e-3 [m^3]         "volume while the piston rests on the stops [m^3]; 30 L"
T_1 = 35 [C]                "initial temperature of the saturated water [°C]"
p_2 = 300e3 [Pa]            "pressure that just lifts the piston [Pa]; 300 kPa in the original"
Vol_3 = 75e-3 [m^3]         "volume when the piston meets the spring [m^3]; 75 L"
p_4 = 7000e3 [Pa]           "final pressure [Pa]; 7000 kPa (7 MPa) in the original"

"State 1: the piston rests on the stops. Saturated mixture, so the pressure is the saturation pressure at T_1."
p_1 = P_sat(Water,T=T_1)    "saturation pressure at 35 °C [Pa]"
v_1 = Vol_1/m_eau           "initial specific volume of the saturated water [m^3/kg]"
x_1 = quality(water,v=v_1,T=T_1)  "initial quality [-]"

"The initial pressure is below 300 kPa, so the first stage is at constant volume until the piston lifts."
W_12 = 0.0                  "boundary work of the constant-volume stage [J]"

"State 2: pressure has reached 300 kPa and the piston is about to leave the stops."
Vol_2 = Vol_1               "volume at lift-off, still the stop volume [m^3]"
v_2 = Vol_2/m_eau           "specific volume at the end of the constant-volume stage [m^3/kg]"
T_2 = temperature(water,p=p_2,v=v_2)  "temperature at lift-off [°C]"
x_2 = quality(water,p=p_2,v=v_2)      "quality at lift-off [-]"

"Second stage: constant pressure at 300 kPa until the volume is 75 L, where the piston meets the spring."
DELTAV_23 = Vol_3 - Vol_2   "volume change of the constant-pressure stage [m^3]"
W_23 = p_2*DELTAV_23        "absolute boundary work of the constant-pressure stage [J], as in the original"

"State 3: the piston has just reached the spring. h_3 is the piston height, not an enthalpy."
h_3 = Vol_3 / A_p           "piston height when the spring is first touched [m]"
p_3 = p_2                   "pressure at spring contact [Pa]"
v_3 = Vol_3/m_eau           "specific volume at spring contact [m^3/kg]"
T_3 = temperature(water,p=p_3,v=v_3)  "temperature at spring contact [°C]"
x_3 = quality(water,p=p_3,v=v_3)      "quality at spring contact [-]"

"Third stage: the external force is known. The spring force sets the pressure-volume law.
 P = F/A = p_2 + k_ressort * DELTA_h / A_p = p_2 + k_ressort * DELTA_V / A_p^2,
 with DELTA_h = h - h_3 and DELTA_V = V - Vol_3."

"Final cylinder volume at 7 MPa. The pressure rise above 300 kPa is balanced by the spring force F = k_ressort * DELTA_h."
DELTAp_34 = p_4-p_3                          "pressure rise while the spring is compressed [Pa]"
DELTAp_34 = k_ressort/A_p*(h_4-h_3)          "same rise from the spring; solves the final piston height h_4 [m]"
Vol_4 = A_p*h_4                              "final volume [m^3]"

"Boundary work: integral of P dV from Vol_3 to Vol_4, again as a positive value.
 P dV = (p_2 + k_ressort/A_p^2 * (V - Vol_3)) dV
 antiderivative: p_2*V + k_ressort/A_p^2 * 0.5 * (V - Vol_3)^2"
W_34 = p_2*(Vol_4-Vol_3) + k_ressort/A_p^2 *0.5* ( (Vol_4-Vol_3)^2 - 0.0 )  "absolute boundary work of the spring stage [J]"

"Final state of the water. Pressure, mass and volume are known."
v_4 = Vol_4/m_eau                     "final specific volume [m^3/kg]"
T_4 = temperature(water,p=p_4,v=v_4)  "final temperature [°C]"
x_4 = quality(water,p=p_4,v=v_4)      "final quality [-]; the original reads this as superheated vapour"
"The final state is superheated vapour. In EES, quality returns 100 outside the dome."

"Total work delivered during the process, negative for the fluid, as in the original."
W = - (W_12 + W_23 + W_34)            "total boundary work [J]"

"The four states, for a Clapeyron diagram, as in the original equation window."
p[1]  = p_1
v[1]  = v_1
p[2]  = p_2
v[2]  = v_2
p[3]  = p_3
v[3]  = v_3
p[4]  = p_4
v[4]  = v_4
